    "verdict": "FIBRE PER-PARITY ACTION TEST (accelerator prerequisite; scope-(a)). Splitting the D-ladder by k-parity (M2 split) and measuring each half's own least-term action point-by-point on the fibre (interior N*, no Padé) over the both-interior window: 24/24 points have A_odd~A_even to rel<2% (worst rel=0.0106). => SINGLE-LEVEL: the two k-parity halves share ONE COMMON leading Borel action at every resolved |u~| (A_odd(|u~|)=A_even(|u~|) across the window; both drift together with |u~| as the shared finite-N* running). The non-commutative two-critical-time Écalle accelerator is therefore NOT the required tool -- a single-level Borel/Écalle resummation on the fibre branch suffices. This resolves the M2-vs-#3861 tension toward single-level: M2's distinct divergence CLASSES (C_odd~0.052, C_even~0.115) are sub-leading (k*log k) coefficients, NOT distinct leading actions (which would differ by O(1), not O(0.0001)). KMAX-converged: identical in-band under the ORD 63->83 (k=31->41) extension. Caveat: interior N* is modest (3..~34), so optimal-truncation asymptotics carry a finite-N* correction (which both parities share -- that is WHY they co-drift). No fit; no c_k wired; nothing to production; no tier change."
